React — Components & Data Flow

Brief Concepts


1. Separate Component Files

React components can be placed in separate files instead of putting
everything in one file.

For example:

App.jsx
Header.jsx
ProductCard.jsx
ProductList.jsx

A component can be exported from one file and imported into another.

This helps keep a React project organized and makes components easier
to reuse.


2. Props

Props are information passed from a parent component to a child
component.

In this example, App is the PARENT and Greeting is the CHILD.

The components are in separate files:

App.jsx
Greeting.jsx


App.jsx — Parent

function App() {
  return <Greeting name="Alex" />;
}

App passes "Alex" to Greeting as a prop.

The prop is called name.


Greeting.jsx — Child

function Greeting(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}</h1>;
}

The child receives the information through props.

The data flow is:

App.jsx (Parent)
    |
    | name="Alex"
    ↓
Greeting.jsx (Child)


Props are read-only. The child should not directly change the props
it receives.


3. props.children

props.children contains the content placed between a component's
opening and closing tags.

In this example, App is the PARENT and Card is the CHILD.

The components are in separate files:

App.jsx
Card.jsx


App.jsx — Parent

function App() {
  return (
    <Card>
      <h2>My Product</h2>
      <p>Price: $100</p>
    </Card>
  );
}

App places the content between <Card> and </Card>.


Card.jsx — Child

function Card(props) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      {props.children}
    </div>
  );
}

The content inside <Card> becomes props.children.

So:

<Card>
  <h2>My Product</h2>
</Card>

means that the <h2> element becomes:

props.children


The component relationship is:

App.jsx (Parent)
    |
    | sends content
    ↓
Card.jsx (Child)
    |
    | displays props.children
    ↓
<h2>My Product</h2>


This makes the Card component reusable because different content
can be placed inside it.


4. Callback Props

A callback prop is a function that a parent component passes to a
child component.

The child can call this function when something happens, such as
when a button is clicked.

In this example, App is the PARENT and Button is the CHILD.

The components are in separate files:

App.jsx
Button.jsx


App.jsx — Parent

function App() {
  function handleClick() {
    console.log("The button was clicked!");
  }

  return <Button onButtonClick={handleClick} />;
}

App passes the handleClick function to Button as a prop.

The prop is called onButtonClick.


Button.jsx — Child

function Button(props) {
  return (
    <button onClick={props.onButtonClick}>
      Click
    </button>
  );
}

Button receives the function through props.

When the button is clicked, it calls:

props.onButtonClick()

This causes handleClick() in the parent, App, to run.


The data flow is:

App.jsx (Parent)
    |
    | passes function as a prop
    ↓
Button.jsx (Child)
    |
    | calls the function
    ↓
App.jsx (Parent)


The important idea is that the child does not directly change the
parent's data.

Instead, the parent gives the child a function that the child can
call.

This is called a callback prop.


5. Lifting State Up

When multiple components need the same state, the state can be
moved to their nearest common parent.

The parent stores the state and passes the necessary data or
functions to the child components.

For example, there are three separate files:

App.jsx
NameInput.jsx
NameDisplay.jsx


App.jsx — Parent

function App() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return (
    <>
      <NameInput name={name} setName={setName} />
      <NameDisplay name={name} />
    </>
  );
}


NameInput.jsx — Child

function NameInput(props) {
  return (
    <input
      value={props.name}
      onChange={e => props.setName(e.target.value)}
    />
  );
}


NameDisplay.jsx — Child

function NameDisplay(props) {
  return <h2>Hello, {props.name}</h2>;
}


App stores the state because both NameInput and NameDisplay need
to use the same data.

The state is moved to their common parent, App.

This is called lifting state up.


6. Conditional Rendering

Conditional rendering means displaying different UI depending on
a condition.

Example:

function LoginMessage({ isLoggedIn }) {
  if (isLoggedIn) {
    return <h2>Welcome!</h2>;
  }

  return <h2>Please log in.</h2>;
}

If isLoggedIn is true, React displays:

Welcome!

Otherwise, React displays:

Please log in.


Another common example:

{isLoggedIn ? <Profile /> : <Login />}

A condition can decide which component should appear.


7. Lists and Keys

React can display multiple components from an array by using
JavaScript's .map().

For example, suppose the project has these separate files:

App.jsx
SongList.jsx
SongCard.jsx


App.jsx — Parent

const songs = [
  { id: 1, title: "Song A" },
  { id: 2, title: "Song B" },
  { id: 3, title: "Song C" }
];

function App() {
  return <SongList songs={songs} />;
}

App passes the song array to SongList using props.


SongList.jsx — Child

function SongList({ songs }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {songs.map(song => (
        <SongCard
          key={song.id}
          title={song.title}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

SongList uses .map() to create one SongCard for every song.


SongCard.jsx — Child

function SongCard({ title }) {
  return <h2>{title}</h2>;
}


The .map() creates one SongCard for every item in the array.

The key helps React identify each item in the list.

When possible, use a unique ID from the data as the key instead of
using the array index.


